GETGRENT(3)                    P32W Unix                     GETGRENT(3)

NAME
       getgrent, getgrnam, getgrgid - group file entries

SYNOPSIS
       #include <grp.h>

       struct group *getgrnam(const char *name);
       struct group *getgrgid(int gid);

       struct group {
           char *gr_name;      /* group name */
           char *gr_passwd;    /* password (not used) */
           int gr_gid;         /* group id */
           char **gr_mem;      /* member names, NULL-ended */
       };

DESCRIPTION
       These read the group file /etc/group, whose lines are

           name:password:gid:member,member,...

       (group(5)). getgrnam returns the first entry with the group
       name name, getgrgid the first with group id gid. Each opens
       the file, reads it from the start, and closes it.

       gr_mem lists the users named on the line (at most 31), ended
       by a NULL; it is empty when the line lists none.

RETURN VALUE
       A pointer to the entry, or NULL if none matches or the file
       cannot be opened. The entry and its strings are static,
       overwritten by the next call.

EXAMPLES
       struct group *gr = getgrgid(getgid());
       printf("%s\n", gr ? gr->gr_name : "?");

FILES
       /etc/group

SEE ALSO
       id(1), chown(1), getpwent(3), group(5)

BUGS
       There is no getgrent itself, nor setgrent or endgrent: the
       page bears V7's name, but a program cannot step through the
       file entry by entry. Lines are not checked for comments or
       blank lines; one longer than 255 characters is cut in
       pieces. There is no initgroups or getgroups: a process has
       one group.
